** =====================================================================
**  BLANK CALCULIX INPUT DECK  -  fill in every <...> and delete the notes
**  Run with:   ccx <jobname>      (job name = file name without .inp)
** =====================================================================
**
** HOW AN INPUT DECK IS BUILT
**   * Lines starting with ** are comments.
**   * A line starting with * is a KEYWORD; after a comma come PARAMETERS
**     (TYPE=..., NSET=..., MATERIAL=...).
**   * The lines that follow a keyword are its DATA (comma separated).
**   * Order matters: nodes -> elements -> material -> section -> step.
**   * Degrees of freedom: 1 = x, 2 = y.  2D elements lie in the x-y plane.
**   * No units: choose a consistent set (e.g. N, mm, MPa).
**   * Everything before *STEP is the MODEL; everything between *STEP and
**     *END STEP is what HAPPENS to it (boundary conditions, loads, output).
**
** WHAT THE DLEARN CARDS BECOME HERE
**     DLEARN input                     CalculiX keyword
**     nodal coordinates ............. *NODE
**     element group + connectivity .. *ELEMENT, TYPE=...
**     material set (E, nu, thickness) *MATERIAL, *ELASTIC, *SOLID SECTION
**     boundary condition codes ...... *BOUNDARY
**     nodal forces (load vector) .... *CLOAD
**     execution control (static) .... *STEP, *STATIC
**     printed results ............... *NODE PRINT, *EL PRINT
**
** ---- PART A: MODEL ---------------------------------------------------
*HEADING
<one-line title of your problem>
**
** Nodes: node number, x, y
*NODE, NSET=NALL
<node>, <x>, <y>
<node>, <x>, <y>
**
** Elements: element number, then its 4 nodes in counter-clockwise order.
** Plane strain: CPE4 (full integration) or CPE4R (reduced integration).
** Plane stress would be CPS4 / CPS4R.
*ELEMENT, TYPE=<CPE4 or CPE4R>, ELSET=EALL
<element>, <node1>, <node2>, <node3>, <node4>
**
** Material: Young's modulus, Poisson's ratio
*MATERIAL, NAME=<material name>
*ELASTIC
<E>, <nu>
**
** Section: links the element set to the material; the data line is the
** thickness (1.0 for a plane problem per unit thickness)
*SOLID SECTION, ELSET=EALL, MATERIAL=<material name>
<thickness>
**
** ---- PART B: NODE SETS (names you use below) --------------------------
*NSET, NSET=<name, e.g. LEFTWALL>
<node>, <node>
**
** ---- PART C: STEP ----------------------------------------------------
*STEP
*STATIC
**
** Boundary conditions: node (or set), first dof, last dof, [value]
** e.g.  "5, 1, 1" blocks the x-motion of node 5; "5, 1, 2" blocks both.
*BOUNDARY
<node>, <first dof>, <last dof>
**
** Concentrated forces: node, dof, value  (consistent nodal loads of any
** distributed load must be computed by you, as in DLEARN)
*CLOAD
<node>, <dof>, <force>
**
** ---- PART D: OUTPUT -------------------------------------------------
** Text results go to <jobname>.dat
*NODE PRINT, NSET=NALL
U
*NODE PRINT, NSET=<name of a set with supports>, TOTALS=YES
RF
** COORD = integration point coordinates, S = stresses, E = strains
*EL PRINT, ELSET=EALL
COORD
S
E
** Results for the viewer CalculiX GraphiX go to <jobname>.frd
*NODE FILE
U, RF
*EL FILE
S, E
*END STEP
**
** ---- CHECKLIST BEFORE RUNNING -----------------------------------
**   [ ] every <...> replaced        [ ] element nodes counter-clockwise
**   [ ] supports prevent rigid-body motion in x, y and rotation
**   [ ] units consistent            [ ] set names spelled identically
